      "text": "To find the total surface area of a pyramid with a square base, we need to calculate both the area of the base and the area of the four triangular faces. Here's how to solve this problem step by step:\n\n1. **Calculate the Base Area:**\n   The base is a square with a side length of 10 cm. The base area (SA_b) is given by:\n   \\[\n   SA_b = \\text{side}^2 = 10 \\, \\text{cm} \\times 10 \\, \\text{cm} = 100 \\, \\text{cm}^2.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the Slant Height of Each Triangular Face:**\n   The height of the pyramid (h) is 12 cm, and we have established that the peak of the pyramid is directly above the center of the base. Therefore, the height of each triangular face, which is the slant height (l), can be determined using the Pythagorean theorem. Since half of the base's diagonal lies along one of the faces' edges, the diagonal of the base square is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Diagonal} = \\text{side} \\times \\sqrt{2} = 10 \\, \\text{cm} \\times \\sqrt{2} = 10\\sqrt{2} \\, \\text{cm}.\n   \\]\n   Half of this value is:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{10\\sqrt{2} \\, \\text{cm}}{2} = 5\\sqrt{2} \\, \\text{cm}.\n   \\]\n   Now, applying the Pythagorean theorem:\n   \\[\n   l^2 = \\text{height}^2 + \\text{half-diagonal}^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   l^2 = 12^2 + (5\\sqrt{2})^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   l^2 = 144 + 50\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   l^2 = 194\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   l = \\sqrt{194} \\approx 13.93 \\, \\text{cm}.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the Area of One Triangular Face:**\n   The area of each triangular face (SA_t) for one complete face at the apex involves calculating:\n   \\[\n   SA_t = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{slant height} \\times \\text{base length}\n   \\]\n   Substituting known values:\n   \\[\n   SA_t = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 13.93 \\, \\text{cm} \\times 10 \\, \\text{cm} = 69.65 \\, \\text{cm}^2.\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the Total Surface Area of the Pyramid:**\n   The total surface area (SA_p) is the sum of the base area and the total area of two pairs of faces along with the apex triangle:\n   \\[\n   SA_p = SA_b + 2 \\times SA_t\n   \\]\n   Substituting our calculated values:\n   \\[\n   SA_p = 100 \\, \\text{cm}^2 + 2 \\times 69.65 \\, \\text{cm}^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   SA_p = 339.3 \\, \\text{cm}^2.\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the total surface area of the pyramid is approximately \\boxed{339} square centimeters.",
